# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LegalEasy self-host environment.
#
#   cp .env.example .env      then fill in the three real values below.
#
# docker-compose reads this file for BOTH variable substitution and the
# app/cloudflared containers' env. Never commit the filled-in .env — it
# holds the auth secret and the tunnel token. (.gitignore already covers
# .env; .env.example is the safe, committed template.)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Database ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Host is the compose service name `mongo`, NOT localhost — the app runs
# in its own container. Keep the db name (here: legaleasy) in sync with
# whatever you restore from Atlas; mongorestore preserves the source db
# name, so match it here.
MONGODB_URI=mongodb://mongo:27017/legaleasy
MONGODB_DB=legaleasy

# ── Auth ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Paste the EXACT SAME value that's in the Vercel project's AUTH_SECRET
# (Vercel → Project → Settings → Environment Variables). Reusing it keeps
# every existing mobile-app login valid after the move — the JWTs are
# signed with this secret. A fresh secret would sign everyone out and
# invalidate issued app tokens.
AUTH_SECRET=paste-the-value-from-vercel

# ── Cloudflare Tunnel ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cloudflare Zero Trust → Networks → Tunnels → Create a tunnel → pick
# "Cloudflared" → copy the token it shows. Then add a Public Hostname on
# that tunnel (e.g. app.yourfirm.in) with Service = HTTP, URL = app:3000.
TUNNEL_TOKEN=paste-the-cloudflare-tunnel-token

# ── Optional: Upstash Redis (leave blank) ─────────────────────────────
# Intentionally unset. The live activity feed automatically falls back to
# database polling when these are absent, so a self-hosted office needs
# no Upstash account. Only set them if you later want the Redis fast-path.
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=
